    {
      "id": "LTD-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-breuil-kisin-period-frames",
      "title": "Integral spanning matrix lattices, Breuil–Kisin prism data, A_inf Frobenius–Galois equations, and Hodge–Tate operator power-sum expansion",
      "statement": "Let ℓ be a prime, Γ a compact topological group, and K a finite extension of ℚ_ℓ. (1) Any continuous representation r : Γ → GL_a(ℚ̄_ℓ) has all matrix entries in a single finite extension L/ℚ_ℓ and admits a finite set t ⊆ M_a(L) spanning M_a(L) over ℚ_ℓ whose ℤ_ℓ-span contains I_a and is left r(Γ)-stable; product-tuple lattices carry linear relations modulo ℓ^N with a uniform trace-norm bound ℓ^K. (2) Every finite extension K/ℚ_ℓ admits an Eisenstein Breuil–Kisin prism datum over Witt vectors of its residue field, and every abstract local period-ring triple fits a concrete period-ring core matching algebraic sections, Teichmüller roots, Fontaine logarithms, unramified Frobenius, and the cyclotomic character. (3) For every Breuil–Kisin frame F with integral period matrix Y whose image under the Witt-vector inclusion equals τ^N T and Y Y' = τ^(2N) I_d, injectivity of the Witt-vector inclusion and unit-cancellation cocycle compatibility establish the three integral period equations φ(Y) = Y A_0, τ^N ρ(g) σ(Y) = σ(τ)^N Y ε(g), and E_0^N ε(g) σ(A_0) = σ(E_0)^N A_0 φ(ε(g)), where A_0 and E_0 denote the evaluations of A and E in A_inf; whenever F is de Rham, its rationalized representation is de Rham. (4) Every de Rham representation over L admits an adapted Hodge basis at each embedding τ : K ↪ L with exact de Rham graded multiplicities; a matrix u ∈ M_a(ℂ_ℓ) is the Hodge–Tate operator for scalar z if and only if u θ_D(V) = θ_D(V) diag(z^(p_i − q)); and whenever a lattice (ρ, b, e) reads r, the lattice and target Hodge–Tate operators satisfy u^n = ∑_i (U^n e)_i σ(b_i) for every n ≥ 0.",
      "hypotheses": "A prime ℓ, a compact topological group Γ, a continuous representation r : Γ → GL_a(ℚ̄_ℓ), a finite extension K/ℚ_ℓ, local period-ring data (A_inf, A_cris, B_cris, B_dR), and an integral Breuil–Kisin frame or adapted de Rham representation.",
      "correspondence": "Compact-image finiteness builds left-stable spanning lattices and modulo-ℓ^N precision towers; Eisenstein polynomials over Witt vectors construct Breuil–Kisin data and concrete period cores; unit-cancellation cocycle identities yield the three A_inf Frobenius–Galois equations and de Rham descent; adapted Hodge bases diagonalize the Hodge–Tate operator and expand u^n in lattice coordinates.",
      "dependencies": [],
